mic disease than primary neurological process", "Autoimmune Condition: could explain constitutional symptoms but lacks supporting lab evidence (e.g., positive autoantibodies)"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional alarm signs (fever, night sweats, weight loss)", "Acute neurological changes (visual acuity drop, ataxia)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.18274853801169588, "posterior": 0.2486662928270151, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["35% blasts with pancytopenia (Hb 10g/dL, platelets 109k) and constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) provide moderate support for myeloid neoplasm (B2) an…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.6030701754385964, "posterior": 0.06989895639021085, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["35% blasts with pancytopenia (Hb 10g/dL, platelets 109k) and constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) provide moderate support for myeloid neoplasm (B2) an…"], "evidence_against": ["Diabetes is not strongly associated with myeloid neoplasms (B2), and its presence doesn't exclude this diagnosis. However, it provides minor against evidence f…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.4118560105126996, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Blast percentage alone is more suggestive of lymphoid neoplasm (B3) than myeloid processes, but absence of typical lymphoid features like acrocyanosis or skin …"], "evidence_against": ["Blasts are characteristic of leukemias rather than plasma cell disorders (B4), but this finding alone doesn't definitively exclude plasma cell neoplasms like b…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B3.1", "B3.2"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "lymphoblastic lymphoma"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.09, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Blast percentage alone is more suggestive of lymphoid neoplasm (B3) than myeloid processes, but absence of typical lymphoid features like acrocyanosis or skin …"]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.18092105263157893, "posterior": 0.17911607574991528, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["chronic myeloid leukemia", "polycythemia vera", "essential thrombocythemia", "primary myelofibrosis"]}, "B3.1": {"id": "B3.1", "label": "Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-Cell ALL) with Constitutional Symptoms", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.24711360630761975, "posterior": 0.24711360630761975, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "lymphoblastic lymphoma"]}, "B3.2": {"id": "B3.2", "label": "Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Leukemic Phase", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.16474240420507985, "posterior": 0.16474240420507985, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": ["acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "lymphoblastic lymphoma"]}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2", "B3.1", "B3.2", "B5"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of 35% blasts in peripheral blood with pancytopenia (Hb 10 g/dL, platelets 109,000/mm³) and constitutional symptoms strongly support a myeloid neoplasm with increased blasts over ch…", "summary": "35% blasts with pancytopenia (Hb 10g/dL, platelets 109k) and constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) provide moderate support for myeloid neoplasm (B2) and weak support for reactive leukocytosis (B1), while lymphoid neoplasm (B3) and plasma cell disorders (B4) are neutral or weakly against."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 35% blasts in peripheral blood and constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) support an acute lymphoblastic leukemia/lymphoid neoplasm over myeloid processes?", "summary": "Blast percentage alone is more suggestive of lymphoid neoplasm (B3) than myeloid processes, but absence of typical lymphoid features like acrocyanosis or skin lesions (B3) weakens this. Plasma cell disorders remain unlikely."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss) and pancytopenia consistent with a plasma cell disorder like multiple myeloma despite the absence of Bence Jones proteins in the provided lab resu…", "summary": "Constitutional symptoms alone are insufficient to support plasma cell disorder (B4) without additional evidence like lytic bone lesions or monoclonal protein. Absence of Bence Jones proteins further weakens this possibility."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's pre-existing diabetes (endocrine system abnormality) contradict the myeloid neoplasm hypothesis, as endocrine abnormalities are more strongly associated with lymphoid neoplasms acc…", "summary": "Diabetes is not strongly associated with myeloid neoplasms (B2), and its presence doesn't exclude this diagnosis. However, it provides minor against evidence for B2 due to stronger historical associations with lymphoid processes."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of acrocyanosis, skin abnormalities, or arterial thrombosis in the physical exam argue against a lymphoid neoplasm with increased blasts?", "summary": "Absence of typical lymphoid physical findings (acrocyanosis, skin lesions) weakly argues against lymphoid neoplasm (B3) given their common presentation in blast phase lymphomas."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of 35% blasts in peripheral blood argue against a plasma cell disorder as the primary diagnosis, given that blast counts are more characteristic of leukemias?", "summary": "Blasts are characteristic of leukemias rather than plasma cell disorders (B4), but this finding alone doesn't definitively exclude plasma cell neoplasms like blastoid myeloma variants."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B3", 0.412], ["B1", 0.249], ["B3.1", 0.247]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch B3 (Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts) has posterior 0.412 < 0.75. Dangerous alternative B2 (Myeloid Neoplasm) remains with posterior 0.07 < 0.10 but could still shift. Estimated remaining value (0.72) and unexhausted discriminators (e.g., bone marrow biopsy, flow cytometry) justify continued expansion."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.72, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.2486662928270151, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Acute myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Chronic myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'leukemia', 'blood', 'elevated red blood cell count', 'elevated white blood cell count'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B3", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.412}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "posterior": 0.249, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3.1", "B5", "B3.2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=83%, source=both]\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-Cell ALL) with Constitutional Symptoms:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, cellular immunodeficiency\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Leukemic Phase:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, cellular immunodeficiency, chronic lymphatic leukemia\n\nPlasma Cell Disorder vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nPlasma Cell Disorder vs Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-Cell ALL) with Constitutional Symptoms:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, cellular immunodeficiency\n\nPlasma Cell Disorder vs Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Leukemic Phase:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, cellular immunodeficiency, chronic lymphatic leukemia\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase) vs Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-Cell ALL) with Constitutional Symptoms:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, bone pain\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase) vs Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Leukemic Phase:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, cellular immunodeficiency, somatic mutation\n\nAcute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-Cell ALL) with Constitutional Symptoms vs Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Leukemic Phase:\n  Favours first:  balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, bone pain, enlarged liver\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Plasma Cell Disorder: hematological disease associated with an acquired peripheral neuropathy, myeloid neoplasm, non-secretory plasma cell myeloma\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase): bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\nRelated to Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-Cell ALL) with Constitutional Symptoms: acute lymphoblastic leukemia (disease), acute lymphoblastic/lymphocytic leukemia, childhood leukemia\n\nRelated to Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Leukemic Phase: non-hodgkin lymphoma, precursor lymphoblastic lymphoma/leukemia, t-cell childhood lymphoblastic lymphoma\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  and night sweats → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  and night sweats → fatigue → Plasma Cell Disorder\n  he experienced a headache with blurry vision → seizure → Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)"}
